---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const withBase = (p = '') => (base.endsWith('/') ? base : base + '/') + p;
---

<Guide
  title="What the Bhagavad Gita says about heartbreak and breakups"
  description="Missing someone and can't stop thinking about them? What the Bhagavad Gita says about pain that passes, a mind that keeps returning, and healing — verses 2.14, 6.26, 6.5 and 18.37."
  slug="bhagavad-gita-on-heartbreak"
  datePublished="2026-10-29"
  faq={[
    {
      q: 'What does the Bhagavad Gita say about heartbreak?',
      a: 'It does not dismiss the pain. Verse 2.14 says pleasure and pain have a beginning and an end and asks us to endure them bravely — the ache is real, and it is not permanent.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How do I stop thinking about my ex, according to the Gita?',
      a: 'Verse 6.26 offers a gentle method: whenever the mind wanders off, notice it and bring it back — again and again, without fighting it. Verse 6.35 admits this is hard and says it comes with practice.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is there a Gita verse about healing?',
      a: 'Verse 18.37 describes a kind of happiness that is “like poison at first but in the end like nectar” — the harder, cleaner path that hurts now and heals later, often exactly what moving on feels like.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Heartbreak has a particular cruelty: the person who used to comfort you is the one you most want
    to talk to about it. The Bhagavad Gita isn’t a book about romance, but it is a book about losing
    what you were attached to and finding your feet again — and it speaks to that with real
    tenderness.
  </p>

  <h2>It hurts, and it will not hurt like this forever</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"The contacts of the senses with the objects, O son of Kunti, which cause heat and cold, pleasure and pain, have a beginning and an end; they are impermanent; endure them bravely, O Arjuna."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/14')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.14</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    The Gita doesn’t tell you the pain is unreal or that you should be over it already. It says pain,
    like every feeling, has a beginning and an end. Enduring it bravely isn’t pretending to be fine —
    it’s letting it move through you without deciding it is permanent.
  </p>

  <h2>When your mind keeps going back</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"From whatever cause the restless and unsteady mind wanders away, from that let him restrain it and bring it under the control of the Self alone."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/6/26')}>Bhagavad Gita 6.26</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Rereading old messages, checking their stories, replaying the last conversation — the mind returns
    to the wound. The Gita’s method is not to scold yourself for it, but to notice and gently come
    back, as many times as it takes. Elsewhere it admits that the mind is “difficult to control and
    restless; but by practice and by dispassion it may be restrained” (<a href={withBase('gita/6/35')}>6.35</a>).
    Every return counts.
  </p>

  <h2>Be on your own side</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"One should raise oneself by one's Self alone; let not one lower oneself; for the Self alone is the friend of oneself, and the Self alone is the enemy of oneself."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/6/5')}>Bhagavad Gita 6.5</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    After a breakup, the voice that says “you weren’t enough” can be loud. The Gita reminds you that you
    can be your own friend in this, not your own enemy. Talk to yourself the way you’d talk to a friend
    going through the same thing.
  </p>

  <h2>Bitter now, sweet later</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"That which is like poison at first but in the end like nectar — that happiness is declared to be Sattvic, born of the purity of one's own mind due to Self-realisation."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/18/37')}>Bhagavad Gita 18.37</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Some choices hurt at first and heal in the end — not texting back, letting go, building a life
    that stands on its own. The Gita calls that the truer kind of happiness. It rarely feels like
    happiness at the start.
  </p>

  <p>
    If what you’re feeling is closer to deep sadness or hopelessness, read our guide on
    <a href={withBase('guides/bhagavad-gita-on-depression')}>despair</a>, and please talk to someone you
    trust. For more on the difference between love and attachment, see
    <a href={withBase('guides/bhagavad-gita-on-love')}>love and relationships</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
